---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CompressLab from "../../components/CompressLab.astro";
import DedupLab from "../../components/DedupLab.astro";
import CryptLab from "../../components/CryptLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1, D28.1): the question, a plain
// answer, the figures, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in
// the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("compression-dedup-encryption");
const Z = "module/zfs/zio.c";
const DDT = "module/zfs/ddt.c";
const SPA = "include/sys/spa.h";
const CRYPT = "module/os/linux/zfs/zio_crypt.c";
const L = {
  execute: gh(Z, 2452, 2454),
  compressData: gh("module/zfs/zio_compress.c", 148, 149),
  bpe: gh(SPA, 356, 357),
  minAlloc: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 428, 437),
  gcd: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 1523, 1536),
  dedupEmpty: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 2465, 2472),
  embed: gh(Z, 2005, 2017),
  embeddedLayout: gh(SPA, 265, 323),
  roundup: gh(Z, 1783, 1789),
  asize: gh("module/zfs/vdev.c", 368, 370),
  holeBirth: gh(Z, 2107, 2115),
  overQuota: gh(DDT, 1136, 1163),
  ddtLookup: gh(DDT, 1374, 1387),
  quotaComment: gh(DDT, 113, 127),
  fill: gh(DDT, 724, 726),
  addref: gh(DDT, 880, 888),
  rewrite: gh(Z, 3971, 3981),
  childWrite: gh(Z, 4110, 4114),
  extend: gh(Z, 3780, 3789),
  key: gh("include/sys/ddt.h", 95, 105),
  value: gh("include/sys/ddt.h", 150, 169),
  log: gh(DDT, 144, 156),
  logFlags: gh(DDT, 303, 310),
  quotaAuto: gh("module/zcommon/zpool_prop.c", 143, 145),
  encodeParams: gh(CRYPT, 751, 773),
  encodeMac: gh(CRYPT, 809, 827),
  iv2: gh(SPA, 531, 538),
  macros: gh(SPA, 452, 470),
  noEmbed: gh(SPA, 320, 322),
  writePolicy: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 2523, 2526),
  suites: gh(CRYPT, 39, 40),
  lens: gh("include/sys/zio.h", 129, 131),
  fold: gh("module/zfs/zio_checksum.c", 324, 341),
  scrubRaw: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4841),
  eacces: gh("module/zfs/dsl_crypt.c", 2818, 2823),
  clear: gh(CRYPT, 115, 120),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="compression-dedup-encryption"
  title="What do compression, dedup and encryption do to a block? · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS keeps a block compressed only if it saves an eighth, counted in whole sectors; dedup looks every block's checksum up in a table; encryption keeps its salt, IV and MAC in the block pointer, so scrub needs no key. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>What do compression, dedup and encryption do to a block?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          ZFS writes files in blocks, 128 KiB by default, and a disk stores them in whole sectors of 512 bytes or
          4 KiB. Before a block is written, three optional features act on it, in this order: compression,
          encryption, then deduplication. ZFS keeps a block compressed only if that saves at least an eighth of it,
          counted in whole sectors; otherwise it stores the block as it is. With compression on, a block of zeros is not written at all, and a block that compresses to 112 bytes or less can go inside its block pointer. A block pointer is the small record a parent block keeps for each child, with the child’s address, size and checksum.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deduplication avoids writing a block that is already on disk. With dedup on, ZFS looks the checksum of every
          block up in a table, the dedup table. If the checksum is there, the new block pointer takes the address of the copy on
          disk, a reference count goes up, and nothing is written. If it is not, the block is written and a new entry
          records its address. The price is a lookup on every write, duplicate or not, and a table that grows with the
          data and has to be updated on disk.
        </p>
        <p>
          To encrypt a block, ZFS needs a salt and an initialization vector (IV), two values that make each block’s encryption different. It also computes a message authentication code (MAC), which proves that the block was not altered. ZFS stores the three in the block pointer, in the place of the third copy’s address and of half of the checksum, with a few bits in a counter that a data block does not need. The other half is a checksum of the encrypted bytes,
          so a scrub can check every block for damage without the key.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compress">
      <h2 id="compress"><span class="n">1</span> Compress a block</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The figure follows one block through the tests the write path makes. Choose the data, the block size, the
          sector size and the compression. The line under the controls says what happened, and what the choice changed
          is outlined.
        </p>
        <p>
          With compressible data, the output stays under the limit, the solid line at seven eighths of the block
          rounded down to a sector, and the block takes fewer sectors on disk. Already compressed data goes over the
          limit and is stored as it is. All zeros makes a hole, and almost empty data fits in the block pointer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then choose a 4 KiB block on 4 KiB sectors. Seven eighths of one sector rounds down to nothing, so the block
          stays compressed only if it shrinks to 112 bytes or less, and then it goes into its block pointer. The table under the figure shows the same data at the three
          sector sizes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CompressLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="dedup">
      <h2 id="dedup"><span class="n">2</span> Write the same block twice</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each Write button writes a block with that content, in a new transaction group (the batch of changes ZFS commits
          every few seconds). The figure opens after three writes: A, B, then A again, which was found in the table: a hit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write C: C is not in the table, a miss, so the block is written and the table gets an entry for its checksum. Write C again:
          it is a hit, the entry’s count goes to 2 and no block is written. The lookup counter goes up with every
          write.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start again, write A, then press Dedup table over its quota and write B. B gets no entry and is written like any
          block. Write A: it is still found in the table and still deduplicated.
        </p>
      </div>
      <DedupLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="crypt">
      <h2 id="crypt"><span class="n">3</span> Unload the key, then scrub</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On the left are the 16 words of a block pointer. For an encrypted data block, the highlighted words are the
          ones that differ from an ordinary block: the third address, the upper half of the fill count and the second
          half of the checksum.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the right is a small encrypted file: its dnode block, which describes the file, an indirect block that
          points to the data, and three data blocks. Press Key loaded to unload the key: the data shows as ciphertext. Scrub: every
          block still passes. Read the file: the read fails at the first data block, which cannot be decrypted. Flip a
          bit, then scrub again: the damaged block is caught, with or without the key.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CryptLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figures follow ten places in the OpenZFS source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release
        zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The order of the stages</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Each stage of a write is one bit, and a write runs its stages from the lowest bit up. Compression comes before
          encryption, and the checksum is computed on the bytes that will be written. The dedup lookup comes after,
          so it uses the checksum of the compressed and encrypted block.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>enum zio_stage</code>, zio_impl.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="include/sys/zio_impl.h"
            from={135}
            to={144}
            symbol="enum zio_stage"
            notes={[
              { from: 135, to: 138, text: "Compression, then encryption, then the checksum of what will be written." },
              { from: 140, text: "Nop-write, which skips a write whose new checksum equals the old one. The figures leave it out." },
              { from: 142, to: 144, text: `The dedup stages. A write moves <a href="${L.execute}">to the next bit set in its pipeline</a>, so they come last.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The limit</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Before compressing, <code>zio_get_compression_max_size</code> works out how big the output may be: seven
          eighths of the block, rounded down to a whole number of sectors. A saving smaller than a sector saves nothing,
          because a block takes whole sectors on disk.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_get_compression_max_size</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={1797}
            to={1807}
            symbol="zio_get_compression_max_size"
            notes={[
              { from: 1797, to: 1798, text: "Seven eighths of the source: at least 12.5% must be saved, a “legacy value”, says the comment." },
              { from: 1800, to: 1802, text: "For ZLE, which only compresses runs of zeros, the limit is not rounded." },
              { from: 1804, text: `Rounded down to a multiple of <code>spa_gcd_alloc</code>, which on a pool of plain disks of one ashift is <a href="${L.minAlloc}">the sector</a> (<a href="${L.gcd}">the greatest common divisor of the vdevs’ allocation sizes</a>).` },
              { from: 1806, to: 1807, text: `If that leaves less than one sector, only an output that fits in a block pointer is worth keeping: <code>BPE_PAYLOAD_SIZE</code>, <a href="${L.bpe}">14 words of 8 bytes</a>, 112 bytes.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Zeros, too big, or small enough to embed</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>zio_write_compress</code> first checks for a block of zeros, then runs the compressor with the limit as
          its output size. The outcome is decided by the size it gets back.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_write_compress</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={1986}
            to={2004}
            symbol="zio_write_compress"
            notes={[
              { from: 1986, to: 1987, text: `All zeros: PSIZE 0. This test runs only with compression on. With compression off and dedup on, ZFS <a href="${L.dedupEmpty}">turns on a mode that only detects zeros</a>, because “storing many references to an all zeros block in the dedup table would be expensive”.` },
              { from: 1991, to: 1995, text: `The compressor gets the limit as its output size; <a href="${L.compressData}">it returns the source size</a> when the output does not fit.` },
              { from: 1996, to: 1997, text: `PSIZE 0 makes a hole: nothing is allocated. If the block existed before, the hole <a href="${L.holeBirth}">keeps its size and is born in this txg</a>, so an incremental send sees it.` },
              { from: 1998, to: 2001, text: "Nothing saved: compression is off for this block, which is stored at its full size." },
              { from: 2002, to: 2004, text: `At most 112 bytes, not encrypted, at level 0, with <code>embedded_data</code> enabled: the data is <a href="${L.embed}">encoded into the block pointer</a>, whose <a href="${L.embeddedLayout}">words then hold the data</a>, and the write stops there.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Rounding up to the sector</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Any other output is rounded up to whole sectors. If the rounded size is not smaller than the block, the
          block is stored uncompressed after all.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_write_compress</code>, rounding, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={2027}
            to={2035}
            symbol="zio_write_compress"
            notes={[
              { from: 2027, to: 2028, text: `<code>zio_roundup_alloc_size</code> <a href="${L.roundup}">rounds up to the minimum allocation, or to a multiple of the sector above it</a>.` },
              { from: 2029, to: 2032, text: "Not smaller than the block: stored uncompressed. The rounded-down limit makes this rare; ZLE, whose limit is not rounded, can reach it, and so can a 112-byte output of an encrypted block, which cannot be embedded." },
              { from: 2034, to: 2035, text: `The tail of the last sector is zeroed and PSIZE is the rounded size, so the <code>compressratio</code> property counts the padding. On a single disk the space allocated is PSIZE <a href="${L.asize}">rounded up to the sector</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Looking the checksum up</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A block of a dataset with dedup on goes through <code>zio_ddt_write</code>, which looks its checksum up in
          the dedup table. Over its quota, the table refuses new entries, and a block it has never seen is written
          normally.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_ddt_write</code>, the lookup, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={3823}
            to={3832}
            symbol="zio_ddt_write"
            notes={[
              { from: 3823, text: `Every dedup write looks its block pointer up, keyed by the checksum. An entry is <a href="${L.key}">a 40-byte key</a> and <a href="${L.value}">a value of 256 bytes, or 72 in the newer flat format</a>.` },
              { from: 3824, to: 3832, text: `NULL comes from <a href="${L.ddtLookup}"><code>ddt_lookup</code></a> when the key is in no table and <a href="${L.overQuota}">the table is over its quota</a>. The D bit is cleared and the block takes the ordinary write path. A key that is found is still returned: <a href="${L.quotaComment}">only new entries are refused</a>. The quota’s default, <code>auto</code>, <a href="${L.quotaAuto}">sets no size</a> and caps the table only on a pool with a dedup or special vdev; on a pool of plain disks, the table grows with the data.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A hit writes nothing</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When the entry already holds an address, the new block pointer is filled from it and the reference count
          goes up. The new block pointer’s logical birth is the current transaction group and its physical birth is the one the data was
          written in, which is why the figure shows “born 102, physical 100”.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_ddt_write</code>, a hit, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={3983}
            to={3986}
            symbol="zio_ddt_write"
            notes={[
              { from: 3983, text: `<code>ddt_bp_fill</code> copies the entry’s addresses into the new block pointer, <a href="${L.fill}">with the current txg as logical birth and the entry’s as physical birth</a>. A rewrite <a href="${L.rewrite}">first fills the pointer with its original birth txg</a>, and does nothing if that gives the same block pointer.` },
              { from: 3984, text: `<a href="${L.addref}"><code>ddt_phys_addref</code></a> adds a reference.` },
              { from: 3985, to: 3986, text: "The function returns with no write I/O." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A miss writes the block and fills the entry</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A new entry has no address yet. The block is written by a child I/O, and when that write has its address,
          the address is copied into the entry for the next hit.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_ddt_write</code>, a miss, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={Z}
            from={4161}
            to={4165}
            symbol="zio_ddt_write"
            notes={[
              { from: 4161, text: "The new entry’s count goes from 0 to 1 now, before the write completes; it is undone if the write fails." },
              { from: 4165, text: `The <a href="${L.childWrite}">child write</a> runs; when it has its address, <a href="${L.extend}"><code>zio_ddt_child_write_ready</code> copies it into the entry</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Keeping the table on disk</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The entries changed in a transaction group must reach the table on disk, which is stored as ZAP objects. A
          comment in <code>ddt.c</code> says why that used to be expensive. The <code>fast_dedup</code> feature adds a
          log for this, and the <code>dedup_table_quota</code> property can cap the table’s size.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label">The dedup log, ddt.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={DDT}
            from={138}
            to={142}
            symbol="DDT: the dedup log"
            notes={[
              { from: 138, to: 142, text: `Without the log, each changed entry was written back at the end of its txg: the whole ZAP leaf read, one entry updated, the leaf written back. With <code>fast_dedup</code>, changed entries are <a href="${L.log}">appended to a log and flushed to the ZAP a little every txg</a>; only tables created with the feature <a href="${L.logFlags}">get the log</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Where the encryption values go</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A comment in <code>spa.h</code> lays out the encrypted block pointer. The MAC takes half of the checksum,
          the salt and the start of the IV take the third address, and the rest of the IV takes the upper half of the
          fill count.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>blkptr_t</code>, encrypted, spa.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={SPA}
            from={250}
            to={257}
            symbol="blkptr_t, encrypted"
            notes={[
              { from: 250, to: 254, text: `The MAC takes the second half of the checksum (<a href="${L.encodeMac}"><code>zio_crypt_encode_mac_bp</code></a>); the first half still detects damage, key or not.` },
              { from: 254, to: 256, text: `Salt and the first 64 bits of the IV in the third address (<a href="${L.encodeParams}"><code>zio_crypt_encode_params_bp</code></a>), so an encrypted block <a href="${L.writePolicy}">keeps at most two copies</a>. An embedded block pointer <a href="${L.noEmbed}">cannot be encrypted</a>: it has no words left.` },
              { from: 256, to: 257, text: `The last 32 bits of the IV in <a href="${L.iv2}">the upper half of the fill count</a>. The same X bit <a href="${L.macros}">marks three kinds of block</a>: encrypted, only authenticated, or an indirect block holding a checksum of the MACs below. The cipher is <a href="${L.suites}">AES-GCM or AES-CCM</a>, with <a href="${L.lens}">an 8-byte salt, a 12-byte IV and a 16-byte MAC</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Scrub without the key</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          When it checks a block that uses encryption, <code>zio_checksum_error_impl</code> compares only the first
          half of the checksum. Scrub reads blocks without decrypting them, so this comparison is all it needs, and it
          works with the key unloaded.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_checksum_error_impl</code>, zio_checksum.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/zio_checksum.c"
            from={515}
            to={525}
            symbol="zio_checksum_error_impl"
            notes={[
              { from: 515, to: 516, text: "A block pointer that uses encryption, except an objset block, which carries its MACs inside itself." },
              { from: 517, to: 520, text: `For a checksum not made for dedup (fletcher-4, for instance), words 2 and 3 are folded into 0 and 1, <a href="${L.fold}">as the writer did</a>.` },
              { from: 522, to: 525, text: `The MAC words are ignored on both sides; decryption checks the MAC. Scrub reads <a href="${L.scrubRaw}">raw</a>, so it stops here; a read that must decrypt <a href="${L.eacces}">fails with <code>EACCES</code></a> when the key is not loaded. The dnode core <a href="${L.clear}">stays in clear</a> for the same reason.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figures leave out</h2>
      <div class="prose muted">
        <p>
          The compressor’s output is an input of the figure, not computed by a real compressor, and the presets are
          examples. LZ4 stands for every compressor but ZLE, since they all follow the same limit. Compression levels
          are left out, and so is the rule that stops compressing rewrites from the eighth sync pass on. The pool is
          plain disks of one ashift, where the minimum allocation and the sector are the same; RAID-Z and mixed pools
          are not shown.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dedup figure writes one block per transaction group with one copy, at made-up addresses. Dedup verify, checksum collisions, extra copies and pruning, which removes old entries never duplicated, are left out; the dedup log is only described, above, in “In the code”. Scrub walks the dedup table first, so a block referenced many times is checked
          once. The code gives no figure for the memory a table takes per terabyte or for a dedup ratio: both depend
          on the data.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ciphertext, salt, IV and MAC in the encryption figure are stand-ins, not AES; the checksum is fletcher-4
          of the figure’s bytes. The key that encrypts the data is a random master key, stored on disk encrypted by the
          user’s key, so changing a passphrase re-encrypts no data, and snapshots and clones share it. With dedup,
          the salt and IV come from the data instead of being random, so encrypted blocks are deduplicated only within
          one dataset and its clones. Key rotation, which takes a new salt after 400,000,000 blocks, and the encryption
          of the intent log are left out. Where a block goes next is in <a href={roomUrl(lang, "metaslabs")}>Metaslabs
          and space maps</a>; an incremental send picks blocks by their logical birth, so a block deduplicated after a snapshot is still
          sent, as <a href={roomUrl(lang, "send-receive")}>send / receive</a> shows.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="compression-dedup-encryption" />
</Base>

